<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Early human migrations</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Early human migrations were the spread of archaic and modern humans across the continents, believed to have begun about 2 million years ago when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a> expanded out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Homo sapiens dispersed within Africa around the time of its speciation, roughly 300,000 years ago. The recent African origin theory holds that modern humans outside Africa descend from a population that left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> roughly 70–50,000 years ago and reached Oceania by about 50,000 years ago and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> about 40,000 years ago, interbreeding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denisovan">Denisovans</a> on the way. People reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> about 20,000 years ago, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> was settled within the past 2,000 years.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The earliest humans developed out of australopithecine ancestors about 3 million years ago, most likely in the area of the Kenyan Rift Valley, where the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> have been found. Around 1.8 million years ago <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> by way of the Levantine corridor and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> and spread across most of the Old World as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p>Later forms gave rise to H. heidelbergensis, which spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> and produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denisovan">Denisovans</a>. Neanderthals spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Denisovans across Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and Oceania. Homo sapiens emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> about 300,000 years ago, according to dating of finds from Jebel Irhoud in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Early Homo sapiens reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in waves between 130,000 and 115,000 years ago, and possibly as early as 185,000 years ago, as a jawbone from Misliya Cave indicates. These migrations do not appear to have led to lasting colonisation and receded by about 80,000 years ago. The dispersal that led to the lasting spread of modern humans took place about 70,000 to 50,000 years ago. A small group from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, bearing mitochondrial haplogroup L3 and possibly numbering fewer than 1,000, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> strait at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-el-mandeb">Bab-el-Mandeb</a> after around 75,000 years ago, and their descendants spread along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> before 55,000 years ago.</p><p>Migration continued along the Asian coast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and Oceania, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> was colonised around 65,000 to 50,000 years ago. Modern humans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> are certain after 40,000 years ago, rapidly replacing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a>. Along the way Homo sapiens interbred with Neanderthals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denisovan">Denisovans</a>, and Denisovan DNA makes up 0.2 per cent of mainland Asian and Native American DNA.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 20,000 years ago the Last Glacial Maximum forced northern populations into refugia, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was later reoccupied. Conventional estimates have humans reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> between 15,000 and 20,000 years ago, either on foot through ice-free corridors or down the Pacific coast, and details of the dates and routes remain under research and debate.</p><p>Holocene migrations, from about 12,000 years ago, accompanied the shift from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and are thought to have given rise to the distribution of the major language families. Contemporary human populations descend in small part, below 10 per cent, from regional varieties of archaic humans.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Early human migrations” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
